# SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
# Qwen3.8-Flash-Next, a 125B MoE (512 experts, 10 active, 48 layers): every routed expert in system
# RAM, file-backed; attention, the shared expert and the KV cache on the card. Experimental, a side
# experiment: measured for speed on one machine (7800X3D, 64 GB) and in one agent session. Native
# Linux with a lean desktop: 18.7 tok/s, prompts at ~100; under WSL2 with 50 GB: 9-10, ~36.
# See docs/qwen38-flash.md.
# Sourced by config.env after the profile, so a profile value wins over the defaults here.

# Unsloth's llama.cpp, the tree Unsloth Studio ships, built here from its source. Its sparse
# attention runs a banded flash-attention kernel (FLASH_ATTN_EXT_BANDED); mainline 8212c78 and
# master of 2026-09-29 run it dense over a full mask, which needs VRAM at runtime that the buffers
# above leave no room for: it died at ~2.5k tokens of prefill twice, at the same 7 386 MiB this
# tree runs deep with. The source commit is not fetchable from the repo, so the pin is the release
# tarball Studio's prebuilt b11160 was built from, with its checksum. See docs/qwen36.md.
: "${LLAMA_REPO:=https://github.com/unslothai/llama.cpp}"
: "${LLAMA_COMMIT:=a3c12db9dfc9a5bdf93df199ec370e9faf117c69}"
: "${LLAMA_TARBALL:=$LLAMA_REPO/releases/download/b11160-mix-a6922cc/llama.cpp-source-commit-$LLAMA_COMMIT.tar.gz}"
: "${LLAMA_TARBALL_SHA256:=cbd0e8c756da5b2f46df4ddacded46c7733316c44d86a1a5d176fe3ade7b0675}"
: "${LLAMA_DIR:=$BONSAI_HOME/llama.cpp-unsloth}"
PATCH_DIR=""

# UD-IQ4_XS, 87.2 GiB in three parts: routed experts 55.4, an n-gram embedding table 26.8 that is
# read lazily and does not limit speed, the rest ~4.4 on the card. One checksum per part.
: "${MODEL_REPO:=unsloth/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-GGUF}"
: "${MODEL_REV:=38bb39ee97821de2c9009abb7e93950eec396e66}"
: "${MODEL_FILE:=UD-IQ4_XS/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-UD-IQ4_XS-00001-of-00003.gguf}"
: "${MODEL_SHA256:=5ce89370720f8bf90890f439361282104c1aa1482d4013bb9a50923e758e71a4 577a38a2392b40ca2193cea502e1d92f60b8cd370675d308e0ec21885d9daaa7 d4634e6d84f0ebb0940be15c90d3790bf6464e3dea3a1cddc567dc0e83ad8833}"
: "${MODEL_ALIAS:=qwen3.8-flash}"
# The experts are read through the page cache. MODEL_RAM_MB is what it runs with at all: with 50 GB
# for WSL2 the cache holds ~48 GB and it decodes at ~10 tok/s, at 30 GB ~6. MODEL_RAM_FULL_MB is
# the MemAvailable before start from which all 55.4 GiB stay cached and the SSD reads nothing: on
# native Linux 59 290 MiB fit, 56 371 did not (T-040). Preflight warns below it. Measured on CUDA only.
MODEL_DISK_MB=89400
MODEL_RAM_MB=46000
MODEL_RAM_FULL_MB=58000
MODEL_BACKENDS="cuda"

: "${KV_K:=q8_0}"
: "${KV_V:=q8_0}"
# Unlike Qwen3.6, this template reads reasoning_effort (low, medium, xhigh, its default) the way
# Bonsai's does. medium as for Bonsai: at ~10 tok/s every thinking token costs a tenth of a second.
: "${EFFORT:=medium}"
# No MTP: the shared-Q8_0 head gives +0-5 % once the experts are cached, costs ~1.1 GB of VRAM,
# and ran the card out of memory at 64k.
: "${SPEC_TYPE:=}"

# --no-repack keeps the experts file-backed, so the page cache holds them instead of anonymous
#   memory that WSL2 swaps out.
# --no-op-offload runs prompt processing on the CPU instead of copying 55 GB of experts over PCIe
#   per ubatch: less VRAM, and faster on the short prompts of a tool loop.
# --cache-ram 0 and --ctx-checkpoints 4 (-cram, -ctxcp): llama.cpp keeps 8 GB of prompt cache and
#   32 checkpoints in RAM by default, which evicts experts from the page cache. Long spellings, so
#   they pass through Unsloth Studio's parser too (scripts/server-flags.sh).
: "${SERVER_ARGS:=--no-repack --no-op-offload --cache-ram 0 --ctx-checkpoints 4}"
